#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105888 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105458 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "558bc791-1cd4-51fc-9e2c-9efdd063323d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12381" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d8675cb0c59ba915cefe4b0bbe539f1e7e38b5a3ac2fae4bd2bb25e4de96e116.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_{S} x^2y \\: \\textrm{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=\\sqrt2x-y$ and whose domain is a semicircle of radius $1$ centered at $O$ in the upper-half plane, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f\\left(x,y,g(x,y)\\right) \\sqrt{1+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2} \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(\\sqrt2x-y) = \\sqrt2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(\\sqrt2x-y) = -1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}{[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{15}(1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049084}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560056 :question-generator/id #uuid "64977da5-b324-5ad5-9e21-bc0edd08c1a1" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186105888.py"} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c71b041-a5b0-56d2-8fe1-cc54ee267a0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276152} {:answer/id #uuid "8590cb62-5cbb-5e94-9c2c-16ff8eb10539" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276675} {:answer/id #uuid "8a61d3a9-5839-57ff-8d87-b1546352c2cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276729} {:answer/id #uuid "a1c5b22a-5b3a-5677-b5ac-28d338d1e4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276934} {:answer/id #uuid "dc2fba7a-1a96-5d9a-8c16-896732e88f1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277405}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a61d3a9-5839-57ff-8d87-b1546352c2cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276729} :answer-field/id #uuid "6049b82d-c71e-575c-a578-68058d47eee4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7aed349f-e0e0-5f67-bd76-7726539edfdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161146" :question/problem "![](images/36/365c6e6ebc861b9d8276773d5afe39b971096f0de6f07fced2c5a2ea9e035554.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3x\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,3x\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}3{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [{r}^{3}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}(1-0) \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "305a627f-325f-5f3b-8d64-02abe4e04896" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102261} {:answer/id #uuid "70656859-96a1-5e8a-8599-832418c1e00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534502} {:answer/id #uuid "887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{6}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534561} {:answer/id #uuid "8dea7678-d219-511f-b3fe-1db777950957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534573} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b4642d-b2a3-5966-82c9-11412eda5001" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887450bf-a003-50a9-abb2-58774fef157d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\sqrt{6}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534561} :answer-field/id #uuid "223d1d79-de5b-5791-9ab7-fa8e90b4871f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186102258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54f23aef-c157-5234-9df4-3f529af50cc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161302" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x-y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-y)=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{6}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cad042-e3b8-5970-b4bc-1bab2bf4016f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275672} {:answer/id #uuid "3b878478-8e1e-520b-a4db-1818b785afe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275996} {:answer/id #uuid "4d0d09d4-8a11-5a5b-bec2-8c816863a4b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276159} {:answer/id #uuid "79cda5fc-2517-525f-8688-5a79879dccd8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276583} {:answer/id #uuid "d1314b9f-3f1e-5892-ac6b-e18c0c36419f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b878478-8e1e-520b-a4db-1818b785afe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275996} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3fea7f-c3ee-5a03-bdbe-79ed9c6a39ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278088}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5610a24a-7851-5e97-8ed8-8bbb2532b7e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161291" :question/problem "![](images/97/9795c04bf6d4bfa407a7b9176cd5c4b97b3d89bfcc6fff17d1d78190047b3a54.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+y)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}6y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(1)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6y\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{D}\\,6y\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{Δ}6r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\cdot \\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}(1+1) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f9a66d7-87b7-58f9-a076-b54b892923b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275768} {:answer/id #uuid "7c15054a-734a-5d80-aa3f-8135e29198e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276609} {:answer/id #uuid "a3c9e5ba-3a49-5395-9f31-5cbdddda130a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276955} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb0ab77-c652-580f-b8b9-74b7d90211f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277296} {:answer/id #uuid "e186f105-1b84-51e2-8b84-167c98d9a5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3c9e5ba-3a49-5395-9f31-5cbdddda130a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276955} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6f0392-f1ae-5916-94a3-2c01a4575273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86501c09-fb5b-5eb4-8dc1-b7ee80036bf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161300" :question/problem "![](images/01/0148ca16ee12a0f651c91091715b4ec07d95639530defb8ed366bacb33e4e87d.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$ in the first quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}8xy\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8xy\\cdot \\sqrt{6}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{D}\\,8xy\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}8r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{∬}_{Δ}4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}4{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{4}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}16\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =8\\sqrt{6}(1+1) \\\\  & =16\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01ef1d15-ab53-5c1e-ae63-22e77b303a32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275493} {:answer/id #uuid "6732bd4b-5bae-5817-8033-888f275b28fc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276400} {:answer/id #uuid "67a2f8f7-b5b8-58b8-9b71-9fb46c231333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276404} {:answer/id #uuid "770b7a40-3fb3-5938-a6fd-72477b0e9ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276552} {:answer/id #uuid "af7eefd6-d587-5e8f-ac1d-55687b70130a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af7eefd6-d587-5e8f-ac1d-55687b70130a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277049} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c6d5d4c-02e0-5686-9189-c711d926bc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7e7a3fcc-a15c-5e4a-92b5-d91d52ab682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161301" :question/problem "![](images/52/5276afad13c3e52854d33a3ff3404ab401781e08d1888214121cf2d56c0bb304.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=2x+2y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x+2y)=2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}5x{y}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,5x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}5{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta [{r}^{5}]_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =96{[\\frac{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta }{3}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =32(1+1) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278475 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05c6bd5e-0a05-54b2-8760-1af815a21d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275528} {:answer/id #uuid "67245e6c-b985-5f9e-a895-775b667aacaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276399} {:answer/id #uuid "77e3d978-da8c-5d68-b60a-4a8194925e3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276562} {:answer/id #uuid "78ac9cfc-a243-510f-9152-b7aa90fe5547" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276571} {:answer/id #uuid "d19e77ba-76c5-54e5-8b35-467bdaf0f222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05c6bd5e-0a05-54b2-8760-1af815a21d72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275528} :answer-field/id #uuid "455d1d44-da82-5aaf-86ed-4b3d398efe8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277834}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc8526c3-3b66-50ba-a72f-6803fda09a5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161311" :question/problem "![](images/5b/5ba2dbc746b9fe8b36202d31bbeb8193ffb9e747d6ed4c2e2f0f994499f88e8e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-x+\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the second quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=-1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-x+\\sqrt{2}y)=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\,]}_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(-1)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d4b598-7e8d-541e-bbd6-3dbbb07b4ca3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275841} {:answer/id #uuid "4304b64b-94d9-5a07-9565-9ca1566abcf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276068} {:answer/id #uuid "4cf0425b-4482-59ac-acf5-655dac3d6002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276156} {:answer/id #uuid "7347d307-a529-5778-9cc3-b43ce3b89820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276522} {:answer/id #uuid "ec21c7c2-b53f-5a6f-9b9f-2e82eb49b46c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4304b64b-94d9-5a07-9565-9ca1566abcf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276068} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d3d767-8116-56a7-b22f-ca77b6227aaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "23220eb4-f0ea-5f75-a067-2a7409386baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-161318" :question/problem "![](images/5d/5d6cedcc2d8c5a232d8d7ed89f050fa7a545ed0da6075ddb44eec67e065b9e87.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the upper half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=-4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-4)^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y\\cdot \\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,3{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}3{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{4}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =15{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,{[\\frac{{r}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[-{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4272be20-e9f6-5f58-8d65-20ec49dcd88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276060} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9849ff-8b7c-559c-9004-711a9263b908" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e-1)\\sqrt{2}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276484} {:answer/id #uuid "875101c4-2932-5d0c-8dfc-50974a95eb38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276696} {:answer/id #uuid "8ea90df1-70bc-5a3d-bc73-2c004006066f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4}\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276772} {:answer/id #uuid "ec474ca4-06b0-5ff6-8cf7-8b695d4c2c54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277550}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4272be20-e9f6-5f58-8d65-20ec49dcd88c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276060} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2c03fe3-db1e-5523-92c9-13f7cee7e078" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cedd414d-7a92-5f38-8d1c-98c03492493d" :question/math-academy-id "q-161322" :question/problem "![](images/92/9215246969b621002a96dec3aed8a012045b8061371e4c6a1234f54d53de29cc.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x+\\sqrt{3}y$ whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{({r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}{e}^{{r}^{2}}r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r{e}^{{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{e}^{{r}^{2}}}{2}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)}{2}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}({e}^{4}-1)\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278486 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1117310d-a59f-5c9e-afac-07ea4e950362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275629} {:answer/id #uuid "632694f6-bc89-55a5-9c76-587db4efb69f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276376} {:answer/id #uuid "71b9ff33-9fa7-56e6-aa27-7e58aa28fdea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276503} {:answer/id #uuid "c5f23306-38b6-5585-95c9-674db2d3737e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277238} {:answer/id #uuid "e6be6744-6027-57b8-8e56-b5d3f941e930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1117310d-a59f-5c9e-afac-07ea4e950362" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275629} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3963dbe-4605-5d7a-9bdb-2239345ae704" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e935e93c-c375-5e87-b42b-951f2a795ef1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161339" :question/problem "![](images/d1/d19d54d0e76ced24d1bd9f865578b72f2b8d67414c8506499c6ae7b1300a6275.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=-2x+2y$ and whose domain is the left half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2x+2y)=-2,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2x+2y)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(x+y)\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{1+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(x+y)\\cdot \\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{∬}_{D}\\,(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A & =3{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{∬}_{Δ}{r}^{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =[\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415982 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "008daf1a-a433-5118-aad3-61bc2cd82151" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415943} {:answer/id #uuid "11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415945} {:answer/id #uuid "31b4ae36-f11f-5826-afc4-9e1f22cf793d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415954} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac59834-bde8-5e44-938a-c936359f2042" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415961} {:answer/id #uuid "a786601a-73a7-5725-8042-296bce616964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "11622f2c-a064-5989-bfd9-037259b1990a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415945} :answer-field/id #uuid "105048ab-2d03-5b31-abc9-b516f5aaa13b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9c04d8b1-030c-5d18-9c3c-13b32378d108" :question/math-academy-id "q-161316" :question/problem "![](images/0b/0b1b263baf01b692a0344388207c12d3c4dac0f49d11cd4bfa744f2841df2c6b.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x-\\sqrt{2}y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle of radius $3$ centered at $O$ in the fourth quadrant, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=1,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x-\\sqrt{2}y)=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{1}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}\\,\\frac{4xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{ }\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}\\frac{4{r}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{∬}_{Δ}2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}2r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta [{r}^{2}]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\,\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =9[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta ]_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1-1) \\\\  & =-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193323db-4837-5978-b14e-f2ac7216eb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415948} {:answer/id #uuid "6a54bfb9-103b-535a-a0de-5777c5154e7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415958} {:answer/id #uuid "a40290ed-721f-5496-959a-a2ae7e6cf644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415962} {:answer/id #uuid "c8beac62-5cb9-5816-b59e-39200a1a65b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415970} {:answer/id #uuid "cb82683b-e2da-594a-840f-2f2e47c75562" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a40290ed-721f-5496-959a-a2ae7e6cf644" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415962} :answer-field/id #uuid "33f085d7-1a2e-5ed1-949e-eab11615591e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ac50dc50-55cb-5157-8205-32f49dbad931" :question/math-academy-id "q-161338" :question/problem "![](images/fe/fe04241f55bae9dae231be6486a3c53985c8dc4af9e9656787267d9d852f829e.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{3}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $1$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{3}y)=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\cdot \\sqrt{20}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{D}\\,(1-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{∬}_{Δ}(1-{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}(r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534983 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933e24f-9535-56e6-8bd8-463ca459b1f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534404} {:answer/id #uuid "54ae8c91-054f-56c1-8736-87af40558e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534433} {:answer/id #uuid "5efef0b8-d72d-5a49-aec0-94be999fd7a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534457} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c386ae-4bfc-514a-a465-86fbebbe089e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c9b1563-417d-535e-abf6-54a7aa2af98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534614} :answer-field/id #uuid "a911c19c-a198-57f8-8ad5-1e251d33cc93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bae08c0-8385-58de-b3f9-2f81e17da99b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161328" :question/problem "![](images/78/7836860694ae6246dee36a70b86bc7fcd8cf7358d47a10586ea6880759fb1588.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S,\n$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=4x+\\sqrt{8}y$ and whose domain is the circle of radius $2$ centered at $O,$ as shown above." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=4,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂z}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x+\\sqrt{8}y)=\\sqrt{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂x})}^{2}+{(\\frac{∂z}{∂y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{1+{4}^{2}+(\\sqrt{8})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ our domain can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{∬}_{D}\\,\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta  \\\\  & =5{∬}_{Δ}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot r\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[\\frac{{r}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{8}{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{3}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559996} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6f9c08-fc7c-5686-90ec-bd5e19971270" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560003} {:answer/id #uuid "9107f827-2e1b-5ff9-a378-2a6c0345e47a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560023} {:answer/id #uuid "bf504992-846a-53c0-b739-ef1c87c02b87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560036} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ff7344-e51a-5da3-99bd-f7f28794aacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22f90bd6-2168-5537-aea8-850bd47c55e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559996} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d783c00-550c-539f-9270-98d59cd31448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "141fe36a-507a-57e3-9528-7652085c824c" :question/problem "![](images/01/013cdd1ba73ee79ef51d00b7dc9f48488cee2299eb593accadfbf026981169c0.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{32}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{32 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{32 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{64 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186559998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b5106df-61b9-5b24-863c-2ae5e567f591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559989} {:answer/id #uuid "2417f5d3-5389-5044-b2a0-418aeb7dac86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559997} {:answer/id #uuid "58f3e230-b70f-57b4-82bf-17794d911a1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560008} {:answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560009} {:answer/id #uuid "aaa96423-8207-5f28-adff-de3f6375c04d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61fb6062-d9a8-5fb9-8a3d-bc7a0f08254a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560009} :answer-field/id #uuid "92fd1ae2-283e-5689-8e46-7ea5679c7ca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c380e3c-eb26-50bd-a669-b181f14f84c3" :question/problem "![](images/79/79b09a881e6809e097e982b58d81a037d162f6b5820894e172955df1551415ca.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the right half-circle of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ - \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{1}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((1))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((1)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14cdfb2d-5fc7-5150-b284-439e38260d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559994} {:answer/id #uuid "4676e0a2-75d8-5a17-a969-7c99e3fa2152" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560004} {:answer/id #uuid "7ac954c9-a0ef-5924-b0d7-d5aac29af9a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560018} {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560049} {:answer/id #uuid "f6db0de2-0b22-5269-b5c3-7a0ea98a8477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560051}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebbb113c-f187-55e0-8895-4843d66c32f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560049} :answer-field/id #uuid "bb9f6651-4921-50c2-a3b2-e6a39b3ee3ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d921d71-b4a3-5244-8d78-9db05b10437e" :question/problem "![](images/13/137a452f1b378359fdd974b11781dddf1e7e9b469d864699b044eaf1cc1a7c20.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{((r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{8}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{8 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cc3cc91-3f29-59d8-80b6-0509e2fa9b0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559991} {:answer/id #uuid "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559999} {:answer/id #uuid "32785a38-9413-5c2f-86f3-80004ad4a202" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560001} {:answer/id #uuid "e8d0ede3-115f-5b05-9411-95243502c7ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560047} {:answer/id #uuid "e9a86298-3ae4-55b4-9fd8-1c22396d65d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e340e39-8adb-52aa-ac10-fd4448c264f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559999} :answer-field/id #uuid "77e408e6-0de3-55b6-a2e4-474fb0066fcf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4ef90de0-c8fe-5f06-94ae-fa8b5651011c" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0af59f24-43d6-5c12-b0e9-e9ef1fc3b437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559988} {:answer/id #uuid "78db087d-54a3-5074-9702-51a2dfed6bc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560016} {:answer/id #uuid "a2d9c1d2-25db-5084-ac89-6a69bec1929c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560027} {:answer/id #uuid "b1d7b1f1-76c5-5f4b-906b-3b95fe95622c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560032} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e62e88f6-c5ce-5fda-9962-c7bbdfcc6e0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560046} :answer-field/id #uuid "65cba47d-19ee-5caf-9052-3301717925d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79638187-bf06-5eeb-9e92-84390aacec40" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=x - y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x - y)=1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((2))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(2 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560020 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cb0f77c-0203-5719-ab9d-91ce8ca040f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559995} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560026} {:answer/id #uuid "b35d874d-13e5-52c0-93cc-3d0c92d94055" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560033} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4b7fd6-e628-5a99-898f-4ef27ca7dfc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560040} {:answer/id #uuid "faaf8fd0-a189-5a52-a852-f75ff62ece6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3} \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560053}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7490fb-e639-56fb-bc3d-36e00d2fe872" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560026} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e19b400-81aa-5f00-9d55-be29eb1d866d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "873a8289-cea9-556f-b99b-1f305a18b7fd" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fbbb7c260754e6579bedc6e773656ea8defdb0493117f05e0e5f02fab4afb36c.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 1\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(1)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{1}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(1)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{1}{r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((\\frac{1}{2}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =\\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\sqrt{3} \\pi$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0319e11a-534e-5e66-aa6f-c12e5f170d43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559986} {:answer/id #uuid "069dbf70-7416-5aa9-b93b-1c0d75ec550f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559987} {:answer/id #uuid "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560014} {:answer/id #uuid "d1731e24-69f6-5cbf-9e13-2f9603b51d48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560042} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0bb7a-9649-5c57-b6ec-ac42c2d42781" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77bfa40c-0afd-5cb8-aa5c-b34ba65cbdf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560014} :answer-field/id #uuid "779704ff-9be7-5e93-805f-a309e7bb251f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8aec1038-bbbf-5f1f-8cad-aecf57bf997f" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{4 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[2 r^{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{8}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $16 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "315f5b77-c3b2-50de-ab4c-837f464ef698" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560000} {:answer/id #uuid "a55d1153-0d00-5bef-9de8-4db03c5e4271" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560029} {:answer/id #uuid "a7f52bae-425e-5b13-a313-db368e2739f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560030} {:answer/id #uuid "b570d8ce-b35e-58a9-a28f-8963dd0ebe8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560034} {:answer/id #uuid "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d90d1bc8-5ffd-55f7-bcef-454797c5a4db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560043} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f9e3b15-fafe-5f5f-9850-3350e5bcb68b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a43799a6-bf36-5f02-8250-1856789bacb5" :question/problem "![](images/73/73d9ffd3c1ab9ad243b1944fe53a2654f89418f34262e8fd44dcf0ef057e79e5.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the disk of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[\\frac{3 r^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{\\left[((6))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}{6}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\theta\\right]_{0}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((2 \\pi))-((0))\\right] \\\\ & =(6 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((2 \\pi)-(0)) \\\\ & =12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $12 \\sqrt{3} \\pi$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62f81263-c580-52d1-90e1-7fa8854069df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560010} {:answer/id #uuid "654cc51a-56c2-5bce-942c-7269e2a14dab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560011} {:answer/id #uuid "8feb4f72-b6e7-5245-ba1e-f747a1cb18d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\sqrt{3} \\left(2 - \\sqrt{2}\\right)}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560022} {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3dcf5-5100-544e-ae55-56e98c4a35fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560041} {:answer/id #uuid "fe74912e-c4ea-527e-af0a-bd1df787f279" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbe3dcf5-5100-544e-ae55-56e98c4a35fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560041} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a717221-6128-50c0-937b-dab9d64bde09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2b56f89-2065-56e0-ba13-8da185fac240" :question/problem "![](images/81/819f039dfc5397bf393de37bbb17f4cb8888ce53a5370bfdf13d172697ffd2f7.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 4 y\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x - y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the first quadrant of radius $1$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x - y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x - y)=-1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(-1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(4 y)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{4 y}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(4(r\\sin\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{4 r^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{1}{4 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[\\frac{4 r^{3}}{3}\\right]_{0}^{1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{(\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((\\frac{4}{3}))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}{\\frac{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[- \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ & =(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\left[((0))-((-1))\\right] \\\\ & =(\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3})\\cdot((0)-(-1)) \\\\ & =\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $\\frac{4 \\sqrt{3}}{3}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e5d5ea-f579-5871-924d-075a207c9aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c882a41-9fb5-5aa6-bfe4-ddedb1e09450" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559990} {:answer/id #uuid "38dc5a67-0af1-5b0c-9479-a3e6537981d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560002} {:answer/id #uuid "c282306c-0f8e-565e-a571-f8ea4d9770bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560037} {:answer/id #uuid "e291533d-3639-5a90-afba-d6eb272eb268" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\sqrt{3} \\left(-2 + \\sqrt{2}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560044} {:answer/id #uuid "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd01d698-3524-577e-b710-3910ff2c0119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 8 \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560054} :answer-field/id #uuid "f56e79b1-49d0-59b4-9498-4712b7f8d4c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e3db0b63-f6f4-5b4a-a9c1-b1b2cfd6f6dd" :question/problem "![](images/44/44fada7635b47f3f7ef635b0466f21d87c71b52e7f39fddb5d36c16472f4ce82.png)\n\nEvaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\\iint_S 3 x\\,\\mathrm{d}S,$$\n\nwhere $S$ is defined by $z=- x + y$ and whose domain is the quarter-circle in the second quadrant of radius $2$ centered at $O$, as shown above. Give an exact answer." :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ defined by $z=g(x,y)$ with $(x,y)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f(x,y,z)$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\\iint_S f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}S=\\iint_D f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.$$\n\nFor the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(- x + y)=-1,$$\n\nand\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(- x + y)=1.$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_S{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}S & =\\iint_D{f(x,y,g(x,y))\\sqrt{1+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\right)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{1+(-1)^2+(1)^2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =\\iint_D{(3 x)\\cdot\\sqrt{3}}\\,\\mathrm{d}A \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ our domain can be expressed as\n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 2,\\ \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula for polar coordinates, where $x=r\\cos\\theta$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_D{3 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}A & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{(3(r\\cos\\theta))\\cdot r}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\iint_\\Delta{3 r^{2} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\int_{0}^{2}{3 r^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}r}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[r^{3}\\right]_{0}^{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{(\\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\left[((8))-((0))\\right]}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(\\sqrt{3})\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{8 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[\\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi} \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\left[((0))-((1))\\right] \\\\ & =(8 \\sqrt{3})\\cdot((0)-(1)) \\\\ & =- 8 \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral is $- 8 \\sqrt{3}$."}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Surface Integrals Using Polar Coordinates"}]}
